  "details": [
    {
      "problem": "sqlite-utils-json-key",
      "contestant": "harnu-spec",
      "repeat": 1,
      "failedHiddenTests": [],
      "judgeNotes": [
        "Very thorough spec: every request and operator atom is stated with a named helper, signatures, error substrings and a named test or command per AC. The only wrinkle is the loose 'no other flag conflicts' phrasing in R-10, which is resolved in the same paragraph and in S-2/AC-14. Little is left to the builder.",
        "Very thorough spec with nearly every behavior pinned down and a named test or command for each AC. The only atom not fully met is O-14, because memory's existing traceback on non-object rows and invalid JSON is deliberately retained. Wording of messages is left free beyond required substrings.",
        "Very thorough spec: nearly every atom is pinned down with named tests and commands. Remaining gaps are small: no explicit version-bump prohibition, and stdin is covered only through test cases. The AC-23 grep proof is weak on docs content but is machine-runnable, and AC-20 uses cog --check on README.md which the spec itself says has no cog block."
      ]
    },
    {
      "problem": "sqlite-utils-json-key",
      "contestant": "harnu-spec",
      "repeat": 2,
      "failedHiddenTests": [
        "tests.test_hidden_bakeoff::test_key_on_top_level_list_is_an_error",
        "tests.test_hidden_bakeoff::test_rows_from_file_key_on_top_level_list_raises"
      ],
      "judgeNotes": [
        "Very thorough spec with executable proofs on every acceptance criterion and the operator rulings carried through. The main divergence from the hidden atoms is O-7: the spec lets --key pass a top-level list through unchanged, where the sheet expects an error. O-13 (memory table names) is never mentioned.",
        "The spec is very thorough, with a pinned contract, named tests and executable proofs for every AC. It deliberately has --key on a top-level list return it unchanged, which contradicts the O-7 atom (error). Memory table-name stability is never mentioned.",
        "Very thorough spec: nearly every behaviour is pinned, with a named test or command for each of the 20 ACs. The one real miss against the atoms is O-7: the spec returns a top-level list unchanged for --key too, where the answer sheet wants an error (RowsFromFileBadJSON). It also never mentions memory table naming (O-13)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "problem": "sqlite-utils-json-key",
      "contestant": "superpowers",
      "repeat": 1,
      "failedHiddenTests": [
        "tests.test_hidden_bakeoff::test_docs_cli_and_python_api_mention_options"
      ],
      "judgeNotes": [
        "The bundle is very complete: the plan and spec agree on the interface, the error cases and the option combinations, and almost every requirement has a named test or command. The only real gap is that O-13 (memory table name stays the file stem) is implied by test fixtures and never stated. No contradictions found; no explicit acceptance-criteria list, so I counted the goals and Review Focus items.",
        "Very complete spec: nearly every operator decision is stated and backed by named parametrized tests with exact commands. Only the memory table-name rule and the python-api.rst/version handling are left implicit. The 'zero decisions' count is low and there are no internal contradictions.",
        "The spec is very complete, with concrete tests for nearly every rule and exact error-content requirements. Gaps are minor: memory table naming, the explicit no-version-bump rule and the python-api.rst edit. Docs checks are the only non-test criteria."
      ]
    },
    {
      "problem": "sqlite-utils-json-key",
      "contestant": "superpowers",
      "repeat": 2,
      "failedHiddenTests": [
        "tests.test_hidden_bakeoff::test_key_object_value_is_one_row",
        "tests.test_hidden_bakeoff::test_rows_from_file_auto_key_on_top_level_list_is_a_no_op",
        "tests.test_hidden_bakeoff::test_top_level_list_unchanged_with_auto_key"
      ],
      "judgeNotes": [
        "Spec is very precise and nearly every behavior has a named test. It diverges from the hidden atoms on the object-value case under --key and on --auto-key with a top-level list (it errors rather than no-op), and never mentions memory table names. Several of these are stated as explicit author choices, so they count as contradicted/partial against the answer sheet rather than ambiguous.",
        "Strong spec with explicit tests for most atoms. Its main divergences from the hidden atoms are that a top-level list under --auto-key is an error (O-7 expects a no-op) and that an object value under --key is an error (O-4 expects one row). O-7 should arguably be contradicted, and R-8 is only partly safe because the spec's default path is unchanged but --auto-key on a list errors.",
        "The spec is detailed and nearly fully testable, but it diverges from the hidden atoms on two points: a top-level list with --auto-key is an error rather than a no-op, and an object value under --key is an error rather than one row. Docs are covered, but the spec leaves the changelog to the maintainer instead of forbidding edits, and the memory table-name behaviour is not stated."
      ]
    },
    {
